      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Significant hypoalgesia (tail-flick reflex at 60 degrees C) was observed in rats during the whole period of 24-hour immobilization in cramped cages, as compared to food-and-water deprived and control animals. This hypoalgesia was not antagonized by naloxone (1 mg/kg), however, the animals periodically receiving the drug during immobilization revealed aggressiveness and significant hypoalgesia after immobilization was discontinued (30-120 min observation)."},
